#~
Fake-review detection, statistical step: is the length of a review different between genuine
and fake reviews, and how much does the answer change with the sample size?

This is "Step 3" of a fake-review study (t-test of review length between two groups at
n = 100, 500 and 2000 per group), run on SYNTHETIC data so it needs no dataset: review
lengths (in words) are drawn from two normal distributions, genuine mean 55 (sd 28) and fake
mean 48 (sd 24), from a fixed seed so every run prints the same table. Swap MakeGroup for a
read of your own CSV (Data.CSV) to run it on real reviews.

For each n it prints the Welch t-test and Student's t-test: t, degrees of freedom, p, the
mean difference with its 95% confidence interval, and Cohen's d / Hedges' g. The point it
shows: the effect size barely moves with n (it describes the data) while the p-value falls
fast (it describes the evidence), so a "significant" result at n = 2000 can be a small
effect. It ends with the same comparison as an OLS regression on a 0/1 column, whose
slope, standard error and p-value are the t-test's.

  compile: obc -src fake_review_ttest.obs -lib stats,gen_collect
  run:     obr fake_review_ttest.obe
           obr fake_review_ttest.obe --seed 7
~#

use System.Stats;

class FakeReviewTTest {
	@state : static : Int;

	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		seed := 42;
		i := 0;
		while(i < args->Size()) {
			if(args[i]->Equals("--seed") & i + 1 < args->Size()) {
				seed := args[i + 1]->ToInt();
				i += 1;
			};
			i += 1;
		};

		sizes := [100, 500, 2000];
		each(s : sizes) {
			n := sizes[s];
			@state := seed + n;
			genuine := MakeGroup(n, 55.0, 28.0);
			fake := MakeGroup(n, 48.0, 24.0);

			"n = {$n} per group  (mean length: genuine {$Float->ToString(Stats->Mean(genuine), 1)}, fake {$Float->ToString(Stats->Mean(fake), 1)})"->PrintLine();
			"  {$TTest->Welch(genuine, fake)->ToString()}"->PrintLine();
			"  {$TTest->TwoSample(genuine, fake)->ToString()}"->PrintLine();
			"  Welch is the safer default: the two groups need not share a variance"->PrintLine();
			""->PrintLine();
		};

		# the same comparison as a regression on a 0/1 "fake" column (n = 500 per group)
		n := 500;
		@state := seed + n;
		genuine := MakeGroup(n, 55.0, 28.0);
		fake := MakeGroup(n, 48.0, 24.0);
		length := Float->New[2 * n];
		kind := String->New[2 * n];
		each(r : n) {
			length[r] := genuine[r];
			kind[r] := "genuine";
			length[n + r] := fake[r];
			kind[n + r] := "fake";
		};
		X := Design->Dummies(kind);
		fit := OLS->Fit(X, length, true, ["genuine"]);
		"OLS: length ~ genuine (fake is the reference level, so the coefficient is mean(genuine) - mean(fake))"->PrintLine();
		fit->ToString()->PrintLine();
	}

	# Park-Miller generator, a uniform in (0, 1)
	function : Uniform() ~ Float {
		@state := (@state * 48271) % 2147483647;
		if(@state <= 0) {
			@state += 2147483646;
		};

		return @state->As(Float) / 2147483647.0;
	}

	# a normal draw: the sum of twelve uniforms, less 6, has mean 0 and variance 1
	function : Normal() ~ Float {
		sum := 0.0;
		each(i : 12) {
			sum += Uniform();
		};

		return sum - 6.0;
	}

	# review lengths in words: at least 5
	function : MakeGroup(n : Int, mean : Float, sd : Float) ~ Float[] {
		out := Float->New[n];
		each(i : n) {
			v := mean + sd * Normal();
			if(v < 5.0) {
				v := 5.0;
			};
			out[i] := v;
		};

		return out;
	}
}
